WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.301
Is it raining? Yes, take an
umbrella. No, take sunglasses.

2
00:00:08.521 --> 00:00:13.072
One question, two answers, and
the input decides which steps run.

3
00:00:13.572 --> 00:00:18.541
An algorithm can fork. It asks
one question about its input.

4
00:00:18.761 --> 00:00:23.916
Leaving for school: get dressed,
pick up your bag. Then the question.

5
00:00:24.136 --> 00:00:28.501
Is it raining? Yes: put on your
raincoat and take the umbrella.

6
00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:33.458
No: put on sunglasses and take
a sun hat. Then walk to school.

7
00:00:33.678 --> 00:00:38.647
The steps before and after are the
same. Only one answer's steps run.

8
00:00:39.147 --> 00:00:43.513
Change the input, and the same
algorithm gives a different output.

9
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:49.723
Raining in. The yes steps run, and out
come the raincoat and the umbrella.

10
00:00:49.943 --> 00:00:55.795
Sunny in. The no steps run, and out
come the sunglasses and the sun hat.

11
00:00:56.015 --> 00:01:01.495
A quiz program is the same. A right
answer in: a tick, a point and well done.

12
00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:07.380
A wrong answer in: a cross and a
hint. The input decided both times.

13
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.688
To write a branch, first choose
the question that decides it.

14
00:01:11.908 --> 00:01:17.063
Borrowing a book. The question
is: is the book on the shelf?

15
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:21.788
You look on the shelf whatever the
answer, so that step goes first.

16
00:01:22.008 --> 00:01:25.444
On the shelf: take it to
the desk and scan your card.

17
00:01:25.664 --> 00:01:31.795
Not there: ask for it to be saved.
Thanking the librarian happens either way.

18
00:01:32.015 --> 00:01:37.448
Now test it both ways. A step in the
wrong place turns up for one answer only.

19
00:01:37.948 --> 00:01:42.685
Water three plants. The same three
steps happen for every plant.

20
00:01:42.905 --> 00:01:47.595
Fill the can, pour on a plant, walk
to the next. Those go in the loop.

21
00:01:47.815 --> 00:01:51.856
Set the count to three, and the
loop does those steps three times.

22
00:01:52.076 --> 00:01:55.791
You get the can out of the shed
once, so it stays outside the loop.

23
00:01:56.011 --> 00:02:00.005
Putting the can away happens once
too, after the loop has finished.

24
00:02:00.505 --> 00:02:04.870
To check a loop, unroll it. Write
out every step it really does.

25
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.619
Three steps, three times
round, makes nine steps.

26
00:02:08.839 --> 00:02:13.483
The can is fetched once before and
put away once after. Eleven in all.

27
00:02:13.703 --> 00:02:18.115
Does it do the whole job and nothing
extra? Then the loop is right.

28
00:02:18.335 --> 00:02:23.072
Four plants instead? Change the
count to four. Nothing else moves.

29
00:02:23.572 --> 00:02:28.030
A branch is a fork: one question,
and a rule for each answer.

30
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.848
Different inputs take different
branches, so you get different outputs.

31
00:02:33.068 --> 00:02:37.201
Steps that repeat go in a loop,
and the count says how many times.
